From 687b316b516daa986f528b1646789314882abfb9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 03:24:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(cron): report a gateway-shutdown interrupt as
 shutdown, not lost fire ownership

cron-7c04faf754: the gateway's mark_running_jobs_interrupted writes the interrupted outcome,
which clears the run's fire claim. The run's real claim heartbeat then missed its claim and the
owner gateway's run (fed a reasonless forwarded cron.cancel) stopped with "cron fire ownership was
lost", logged at ERROR with a traceback (the coach's "Job '<name>' failed:" defect line) although
no other process took the claim.

- cron/cancellation.py: ShutdownAwareCancel wraps a claim latch with an in-process "interrupted by
  this gateway's stop" check; reason becomes "Gateway shutdown interrupted the run" and
  shutdown_interrupted is exposed (also through _CombinedCancelEvent). CronShutdownInterrupted.
- cron/scheduler.py: the fire-claim heartbeat latch is shutdown-aware (scoped by execution token)
  and logs a distinct WARNING; _abort_if_fire_claim_lost raises CronShutdownInterrupted for it;
  run_job logs that as one WARNING without traceback (_log_job_failure); real ownership loss and
  every other failure stay ERROR + traceback. fire_interrupted_by_shutdown() for the owner side.
- gateway/session_cron.py execute: the forwarded cancel is shutdown-aware for fires this process
  interrupted.
- tests: real heartbeat through a real interrupt mark (red before: reason 'cron fire ownership was
  lost'), foreign takeover sibling still 'ownership was lost', owner-side execute both ways,
  watchdog/log-level tests. Fakes of _run_with_fire_claim_heartbeat accept the new kwarg.
